{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact head 371f17389de2ff5e1a7771626561857f8d9680e4 for the complete three-part credential cleanup follow-up under request c9cd4cd859e9f715a743a674845e1c7e0cc4b994. All ten artifacts, the request, report, new source/test changes and their surrounding execution paths reviewed against approved main 93a2552e45c5a46c4dcf624e28c7c703d987e114. The prerequisite b2509b23 incorporation is sealed and satisfied. Estimates did not reduce the scope.\n\nArchitecture: affected Artroom layers are the Git snapshot repository adapter, Room core-to-adapter wake wiring, Durable Object recovery/scheduler and job-token inventory consumer. In the gitseq architecture model these remain application and storage integration; no signed kernel or Workroom authority/fold contract changes. Artroom has no docs/reference/architecture.md here; docs/protocol.md is unchanged and remains its public contract. The existing optional internal wake port now forwards to the Room's persisted alarm. Recovery uses the existing earliest-work calculation for founded rooms and the founding ledger before founding. No new public state, schema, token authority or protocol migration is needed.\n\nSnapshot preparation records its create step and unique name before requesting a wake, awaits that wake before provider dispatch, and atomically marks a never-sent step done and removes only its own row if the wake rejects. Existing superseded/legacy debt is preserved. Actual production Room workerd controls start with no stored alarm, hold the dispatched snapshot create, inspect the persisted alarm, abort the object, apply the create late and service deletion through a fresh object's alarm. The failed pre-effect wake sends no create, closes the step as not-sent, and a healthy job retry completes. My independent delayed-wake control confirms that while the wake is still pending the create count stays zero, then exactly one create follows its acceptance.\n\nFresh-object recovery schedules the existing minimum due work in a founded room as well as founding debt in an unfounded one. Actual DO restart controls deliberately remove the alarm before restart and verify the fresh object stores one for snapshot retirement, released-workspace token/inventory debt and an unknown job mint. Running that stored alarm deletes the snapshot, settles the known workspace debt, and observes/reschedules the unknown mint without closing it. The unfounded older-ledger control still passes.\n\nSecurity: the shared completeInventory rule validates total/count and every record's ID, scope, state and timestamp-string expiry before job inventory observations are trusted. Malformed scope/expiry controls fail under the old inline rule and pass on this head. Unknown mints remain open after time passes and even after a clean inventory; canonical inventory is observation only and never authorizes revoking an unattributed token. Known token cleanup retains existing ownership and expiry rules. Snapshot deletion stays fenced to its attempt's name, existing founded repository identity and namespace selection are preserved, and no public token plaintext, new admission/signature authority or unrelated credential operation is introduced. No unresolved defect found within this assigned scope.\n\nSimplification: use one inventory validator rather than a divergent job rule, the existing persisted wake capability rather than a new scheduler, and nextAlarm for founded-room recovery rather than another list of cleanup tables. The short transactional not-sent handler records precisely what is known and adds no unknown-effect state. No material unnecessary mechanism to cut in this change.\n\nFresh independent checks at the clean exact head in /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-followup-v1: root npm run typecheck exit 0; root npm test exit 0, 1978 passed and one skipped, including Git Node 221, Room Node 125 and Room workerd 394; separate Git Worker suite 8 passed; Room wrangler deploy --dry-run with wrangler.spike.jsonc exit 0. Scope/whitespace checks pass. Dependencies were copied from a previously validated installed checkout with unchanged package/lockfile, not independently installed with npm ci. The candidate remained clean.\n\nIn a separate clone, all seven reported guards Q1–Q7 independently produced named runtime failures; an eighth mutation removes the awaited snapshot wake and fails my delayed-wake control. No compile/import failures count as evidence. All modified production files were restored; exact-source replay of the affected DO selection passes eight tests, and the independent delayed-wake control passes. Evidence: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-followup-mutants.json, individual Q1–Q8 logs, /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-followup-target-do.log and /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-followup-wake-final.log.\n\nEvidence limit: the pre-effect fault seam exercises a rejected wake callback. It is not a claim that a failed underlying Cloudflare storage write can bypass output gates. The real persisted-alarm/abort/late-create control separately covers successful storage and host loss.\n\nThe 602-file head preserves 592 paths byte-for-byte from approved main, modifies nine assigned existing paths and adds one workspace recovery test. No dependency or public protocol change. The separate plan 003 driver at 611edea still has changes requested in f060871bfd1610447865b3069b3e48efd6f6c349 for pending-pass alarm churn and completion-error backoff; this head does not contain those landing changes and this approval does not settle that request. Canonical mint attribution and cleanup visibility remain separately tracked. No deployment, live provider resource mutation, real credential creation or inference was performed. This approval binds the exact artifact set; normal ratification and a sealed incorporation receipt remain required.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2cb971aedd46067aac9b50385eac1bc2d1b42f77","binding":"assigned","head":"371f17389de2ff5